The Go binary has two layers: UPX packer and Garble obfuscation. Initial unpacking:
1. UPX unpack via upx -d malware.exe → reveals Garble-obfuscated Go.
2. Entropy shift detection via DetectionLab/entropy.py highlights Garble compression. Use garble-disassemble via tinygo:
   tinygo tool objdump -raw -s .text malware.exe > raw.bin
3. Reconstruct Go runtime in Ghidra using Ghidra-Go-Plugin. Identify Go struct types by analyzing .gopclntab section.
4. Locate RVAPI entry via NtCreateKey / NtSetValueKey hooks in ntdll.dll. Frida script:
   Interceptor.attach(Module.findExportByName('ntdll', 'NtSetValueKey'), {
     onEnter: function(args) {
       var keyName = args[1].readUtf16String();
       if (keyName.includes('HKCU\\Control Panel\\International\\Locale')) {
         console.log('RVAPI write detected:', keyName);
         saveValue(args[4], 'config.bin');
       }
   }});
5. Decode config.bin via xor(0x63) yielding JSON with C2: hxxps[://]api[.]evilcdn[.]com/stats.
Mitigation: Enable HVCI + WDAC policies to block unsigned Go binaries, audit HKCU\Control Panel registry writes via Sysmon event ID 12/13.
Real-world: Mustang Panda used Go malware in the 2023 Asia-Pacific campaigns.

